Sculpt the **Aegis**, a colossal six-legged war-fortress standing at rest,
as a **3D mesh** in a 120x110x150 volume using only the `mc` Marching Cubes tool.

## How meshing works

The only way to shape the surface is the `mc` binary already on your `PATH`. It is
the sole channel: you build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and
the ordered operations you issue are the
**authoritative output that is scored**, not any pixels on disk (the mesh is
extracted to `mesh.glb`). A model produced any other way (writing `mesh.glb`
directly, editing `model.png` by hand) is discarded and flagged; only what the
tool emits counts.

`mc` maintains a **continuous signed-distance field** and extracts a triangle mesh
from its surface. You shape that field by
**compositing solid primitives** — union material in with `add-sphere` /
`add-box` / `add-ellipsoid` / `add-cylinder`, carve it away with the matching
`subtract-*` primitives, blend joins with `--blend`, and reflect/shift/duplicate/
recolor the whole field with `mirror` / `translate` / `copy` / `replace-color`.
Each primitive carries an **opaque** `#rrggbb` color — there is no transparency.
Marching Cubes meshes on a **coarse** grid, so the surface reads chunky and
**faceted**; detail finer than the grid is lost.

The field starts **empty**. After each call the binary re-renders the current mesh
to a 3D preview at `model.png` and appends the operation to its op log. A blank
starting preview is already in place at `{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
mc add-box --x 60 --y 55 --z 80 --width 76 --height 38 --depth 100 --color "#c69a4b"
```

Run `mc --help` to list every operation and `mc <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`model.png` after your calls** to see the extracted surface and decide what to do
next.

